{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790429220,"nonce":"7znz5Fw26eAhYWclYJBw/0wsc9k=","title":"wasm: a Rust frame is not a GC root","message":"On wasm, a value LLVM keeps in a wasm local lives in the engine's frame\nstorage, not in linear memory, and the collector cannot scan it. Ash\npins every pointer-typed register of compiled Haxe to the shadow stack\n(TargetAbi::pointer_registers_in_memory, pin_register). It roots its\nRust runtime code by hand where needed: buffer.rs registers a\npersistent root on wasm.\n\nCaribou's Rust code assumes the native model, where a value live\nacross a call sits in a register the mutator publishes, or on the\nmachine stack, which is scanned:\n- cell::made skips the handle when heap::scans_current_stack() holds;\n- `black_box(obj)` with \"stays live on this frame\" in caribou-wren\n  import.rs, caribou-ash callback.rs, proto.rs and import.rs, and\n  caribou-zyntax foreign.rs;\n- more generally, any Value held in a local across an allocation or a\n  safepoint.\n\nOn wasm every one of these can let the collector free a live object.\nNeeded: a heap predicate saying whether Rust frames are scanned, false\non wasm; handles (or another root the collector sees) at each such\nsite on wasm; and a wasm test that collects under a bridge crossing,\nmodelled on Ash's RootLost.hx.","files":null}]}